{"seq": 1, "type": "user_message", "ts": "2026-11-22T06:36:37.198426+00:00", "payload": {"text": "# Your workspace\n\nYou are in `/work`, inside a Linux container with no network access. Write a Go program here.\n[Maelstrom](https://github.com/jepsen-io/maelstrom) will start that program once for every node\nof a simulated cluster, send it client requests, and check that the answers are correct.\n\nWhat is installed:\n\n- Go 1.27.\n- Maelstrom 0.2.4, as the `maelstrom` command, so you can test your program yourself.\n- Maelstrom's Go library, already in the module cache. Start your module with:\n\n      go mod init node\n      go get github.com/jepsen-io/maelstrom/demo/go@v0.0.0-20260710121306-480a8197026d\n\nA node talks to the rest of the cluster through standard input and output: it reads one JSON\nmessage per line on stdin and writes one per line on stdout. Each message has a `src`, a `dest`\nand a `body`, and the body's `type` says what it is. Anything written to stderr is a log line.\nThe library does that framing for you: `maelstrom.NewNode()`, then `Handle` a message type,\n`Reply` to a request, `Send` or `RPC` to another node, and `Run` to start. It also answers the\n`init` message that tells each node its own ID and the IDs of all the nodes.\n\n# Kafka-style log across two nodes\n\nWrite a node for a cluster of two that together keep many append-only logs, each named by a\nstring key, and remember how far clients have read in each. Clients send any of four kinds of\nrequest to either node, so both must agree on every log: the same offsets for the same values,\nwhichever node a client asks.\n\n`send` appends a value to a log and answers with the offset it was given. Offsets in each log\nstart anywhere you like but must only ever grow, and two values in one log never share an offset:\n\n    {\"type\": \"send\", \"msg_id\": 1, \"key\": \"k1\", \"msg\": 123}\n    {\"type\": \"send_ok\", \"in_reply_to\": 1, \"offset\": 1000}\n\n`poll` asks for values from several logs, each starting at a given offset. For every log it\nanswers `[offset, value]` pairs in offset order, starting with the value at that offset or the\nfirst one after it. It may answer fewer pairs than the log holds, and may leave out a log with\nnothing at or after its offset:\n\n    {\"type\": \"poll\", \"msg_id\": 2, \"offsets\": {\"k1\": 1000, \"k2\": 2000}}\n    {\"type\": \"poll_ok\", \"in_reply_to\": 2, \"msgs\": {\"k1\": [[1000, 9], [1001, 5]], \"k2\": [[2000, 7]]}}\n\n`commit_offsets` records, for each log, the offset a client has finished reading up to:\n\n    {\"type\": \"commit_offsets\", \"msg_id\": 3, \"offsets\": {\"k1\": 1000, \"k2\": 2000}}\n    {\"type\": \"commit_offsets_ok\", \"in_reply_to\": 3}\n\n`list_committed_offsets` answers the committed offset of each log asked about, leaving out logs\nwith nothing committed:\n\n    {\"type\": \"list_committed_offsets\", \"msg_id\": 4, \"keys\": [\"k1\", \"k2\"]}\n    {\"type\": \"list_committed_offsets_ok\", \"in_reply_to\": 4, \"offsets\": {\"k1\": 1000, \"k2\": 2000}}\n\nAn acknowledged `send` must never be lost or changed: a poll that reaches past its offset must\nnot skip it, and every answer for that offset must give the same value. Nothing fails during\nthe test.\n\nMaelstrom also runs a key/value store the nodes can use, as a service named `lin-kv`. It is\nlinearizable: a read always sees the latest write. The Go library talks to it with\n`maelstrom.NewLinKV(node)`, which has `Read`, `ReadInt`, `Write` and `CompareAndSwap` (each takes\na `context.Context`). The nodes may also send each other messages of your own design.\n\n## How it's graded\n\nYou have 45 minutes and 150 steps, a step being one reply from you, however many tools it runs. If either runs out, you are stopped where you are.\n\nWhen you stop, your program is built with `go build -o node .` and tested 4 times with:\n\n    maelstrom test --workload kafka --bin ./node --concurrency 2n --node-count 2 --rate 1000 --time-limit 20\n\nThe runs are independent, each with its own timing and faults. The attempt passes only if it builds and every run is valid.\n"}}
{"seq": 2, "type": "assistant_message", "ts": "2026-11-22T06:36:42.576461+00:00", "payload": {"reasoning": "We need to implement a Kafka-style log across two nodes, using Maelstrom framework and lin-kv store.\n\nKey points:\n- Two nodes.\n- Each node keeps logs named by string key, append-only.\n- Must agree on offsets for same values across both nodes.\n- We can use lin-kv to coordinate.\n- Requests: send, poll, commit_offsets, list_committed_offsets.\n- Both nodes must agree on every log: same offsets for same values, whichever node a client asks.\n- Since there's only two nodes, we need to replicate logs.\n\nApproach:\nUse lin-kv to store log data. Since lin-kv is linearizable, we can use it as source of truth.\nFor each key, store the log entries as a list or as individual key-value pairs.\n\nSimplify:\nFor each log key, maintain in lin-kv:\n- A key for the log: maybe \"log:<key>\" containing a JSON array of values? But updates would require read-modify-write. Since lin-kv has CAS, we can do optimistic concurrency.\n- Or store each entry individually: \"log:<key>:<offset>\" => value. Then append means compute a new offset. But to compute new offset we need to know current max offset. Could read the max offset via a separate key or scan.\n\nSimpler: store log as a JSON array under key \"log:<key>\". For send:\n1. Read current array from lin-kv.\n2. Append new value.\n3. Write back with CAS? But Write just overwrites; since linearizable, if two nodes write concurrently, last write wins and could lose entries. So we must use CompareAndSwap to ensure atomic updates.\n\nBut using CAS for arrays could work:\n- Read \"log:<key>\" => array string.\n- Modify: append value.\n- CAS with expected old value. If fails, retry.\n\nSince only two nodes and concurrency maybe high, retries are okay. But with high rate 1000 and time limit 20, it should be fine.\n\nBut reading entire array for each send/poll could be heavy if logs grow large. However workload likely not huge. Could also maintain a separate key for max offset and entries individually.\n\nAlternative: store entries individually by offset, and maintain a \"next_offset\" or \"max_offset\" per key. To append:\n- Read \"log_meta:<key>\" to get current max offset (or array length).\n- Compute new offset = max_offset + 1 (or start somewhere).\n- Write entry \"log:<key>:<offset>\" = value.\n- Update meta with new max offset using CAS.\n\nBut poll needs to read entries from a given offset onward. Could read a range of keys: \"log:<key>:\" prefix? But lin-kv Read takes exact key or maybe ReadInt? The Go library may not have prefix scan. Let's check.\n\nWe need to know the API of `maelstrom.NewLinKV(node)`. Let's inspect the library.", "text": "", "tool_calls": [{"arguments": {"command": "ls"}, "arguments_error": null, "id": "call_01a201f8a66b79c29690e6a2", "name": "shell"}], "usage": {"input_tokens": 1397, "output_tokens": 624}}}
